-0.00783538818359375, -0.0167083740234375, 0.044189453125, 0.01299285888671875, 0.02386474609375, 0.015625, 0.01499176025390625, 0.014892578125, -0.0032939910888671875, -0.051849365234375, 0.00464630126953125, -0.0129241943359375, -0.004283905029296875, 0.06158447265625, -0.0165252685546875, -0.003482818603515625, -0.01448822021484375, 0.004787445068359375, 0.007106781005859375, 0.0235748291015625, 0.00826263427734375, 0.008880615234375, 0.00585174560546875, 0.005527496337890625, -0.006103515625, 0.0212860107421875, 0.00397491455078125, 0.0183258056640625, -0.004566192626953125, 0.0106964111328125, 0.01085662841796875, -0.01483917236328125, -0.001049041748046875, -0.0106048583984375, -0.014434814453125, -0.0165863037109375, -0.0125885009765625, 0.028839111328125, -0.03192138671875, 0.01763916015625, 0.0081634521484375, 0.019775390625, -0.0147552490234375, -0.005664825439453125, -0.016021728515625, 0.01251220703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nHow level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. In level-k models, \"level-0 actions\" refer to choices made by players who do not engage in strategic reasoning; these actions are typically assumed to be non-strategic, such as random selection or choosing salient or focal options. In contrast, \"level-0 beliefs\" are the expectations that these players hold about the actions of others, which are also presumed to be non-strategic and uninformed by higher-order reasoning. Throughout this paper, we define level-0 actions as the actual choices made without strategic adjustment, and level-0 beliefs as the stated expectations about others\u2019 choices, elicited prior to any strategic reasoning or adjustment. For example, a level-0 belief might be expressed as, \"I think other teams will just pick the middle number,\" or \"Others will probably choose randomly.\" We emphasize that, in our operationalization, both actions and beliefs are intended to capture non-strategic, uninformed reasoning, and we take care to distinguish between the two in our analysis and reporting.\n\nUnderstanding the formation and alignment of level-0 actions and beliefs is crucial for accurately modeling behavior in strategic settings. This study addresses two main research questions: (1) How are level-0 actions and beliefs formed, and do they align within individuals? (2) How do these patterns differ across games, and what are the implications for the assumptions underlying standard level-k models? We hypothesize that the alignment between level-0 actions and beliefs is context-dependent and that standard level-k assumptions may not hold uniformly across different strategic settings.\n\nMethods\n\nOverview and Rationale for Team-Based Consensus\n\nWe employ the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), in which participants are assigned to teams and required to reach consensus on both their action and belief. The team-based consensus format was chosen for two reasons. First, it allows for the observation and recording of explicit reasoning processes, as team members must articulate and negotiate their reasoning aloud, providing rich data for transcript analysis. Second, this format enables us to examine how group deliberation shapes the formation and alignment of level-0 actions and beliefs, which is directly relevant to our research questions about the robustness and perception of non-strategic reasoning. While we acknowledge that group dynamics may influence outcomes, the team-based approach offers unique insights into the social construction of level-0 reasoning that are not accessible through individual elicitation alone.\n\nExperimental Design and Procedure\n\nParticipants were recruited from a university subject pool and randomly assigned to teams of three, resulting in 20 teams per game (40 teams total). Each team played one of two experimental games: the canonical beauty contest game or a variant of the 11-20 game. In total, 120 undergraduate students (60 per game) participated, with demographic information collected (majority aged 18\u201325, balanced gender representation).\n\nUpon arrival, participants received standardized written and verbal instructions explaining the rules of the assigned game. Instructions emphasized that teams would need to reach a consensus on both their action (the number to choose) and their belief (what number they expected other teams to choose). Teams were given up to 15 minutes to discuss openly and reach consensus, with discussions audio-recorded for subsequent analysis. No time pressure was imposed, but teams were encouraged to ensure all members contributed. After consensus was reached, teams recorded their final action and belief on a response sheet. To incentivize thoughtful participation, teams received monetary rewards based on their performance in the game, with additional small bonuses for accurate belief statements (i.e., if their belief was close to the actual modal choice of other teams).\n\nGame Descriptions\n\nBeauty Contest Game\n\nThe beauty contest game requires each team to choose a number between 0 and 100, aiming to guess two-thirds of the average number chosen by all teams. This game is widely regarded as a benchmark for eliciting non-strategic (level-0) behavior due to its simple structure and clear focal point (50), making it suitable for examining the alignment of level-0 actions and beliefs.\n\n11-20 Game Variant\n\nThe 11-20 game, originally introduced by Goeree et al. (2018), involves each team selecting an integer between 11 and 20, with payoffs awarded if a team's chosen number is exactly one less than another team's choice. In our variant, we doubled the reward for achieving this condition and added a small bonus for choosing the lowest unique number. These modifications were made to increase the incentive for strategic reasoning and to introduce additional focal points, thereby testing whether level-0 reasoning persists or changes under more complex and competitive incentives.\n\nRationale for Game Selection and Modifications\n\nThe two games were selected to provide contrasting contexts for level-0 reasoning: the beauty contest game offers a clear focal point and minimal strategic complexity, while the modified 11-20 game introduces more complex incentives and multiple potential focal points. By comparing these settings, we aim to test the robustness of standard level-0 assumptions and to explore whether context-dependent shifts in non-strategic reasoning occur.\n\nTranscript Coding and Reliability\n\nAll team discussions were transcribed and coded to identify instances of level-0 reasoning. The coding scheme was developed based on prior literature and pilot data, with level-0 reasoning defined as explicit statements or arguments indicating non-strategic choice (e.g., \"just pick the middle number,\" \"choose randomly,\" \"pick the lowest number because it stands out\") and corresponding beliefs about others' choices that reflect a lack of strategic adjustment (e.g., \"others will probably pick randomly,\" \"I think most teams will just pick 50\"). Ambiguous cases\u2014such as statements that could be interpreted as either non-strategic or minimally strategic (e.g., \"maybe 15 is a good number because it's not too high or too low\")\u2014were discussed by coders and only classified as level-0 if there was clear evidence of non-strategic intent and no reference to anticipating others\u2019 strategies. Coders also noted statements reflecting strategic reasoning for comparison.\n\nTwo independent coders, trained on a subset of transcripts and blind to the experimental condition, reviewed all transcripts. Discrepancies were resolved through discussion until consensus was reached; in cases where consensus could not be reached, a third coder adjudicated. Inter-rater reliability, measured by Cohen\u2019s kappa, was 0.87, indicating high agreement. Example of a coded level-0 action: \"Let's just pick 50, it's right in the middle.\" Example of a coded level-0 belief: \"I bet other teams will also pick 50 because it's the obvious choice.\" An example of an ambiguous case: \"If we pick 12, maybe others will go for 13,\" which was not coded as level-0 unless accompanied by explicit statements of non-strategic reasoning.\n\nResults\n\nIn the beauty contest game, 18% of teams (4 out of 20) exhibited level-0 reasoning based on transcript coding. Within these teams, the mean chosen number was 50 (SD = 2.1), and their stated beliefs about others\u2019 choices also centered around 50 (mean = 49.8, SD = 2.4). The close correspondence between actions and beliefs suggests alignment in non-strategic reasoning for these teams. Given the small sample size (n=4), we refrain from drawing strong statistical inferences and instead emphasize the descriptive and qualitative alignment observed.\n\nIn the 11-20 game variant, 15% of teams (3 out of 20) were classified as exhibiting level-0 reasoning. For these teams, the mean chosen number was 15.2 (SD = 2.8), while their stated beliefs about others\u2019 choices averaged 17.6 (SD = 1.9). Here, a notable divergence between actions and beliefs was observed: teams tended to choose lower numbers themselves but expected others to choose higher numbers. Again, due to the very small sample size (n=3), we do not rely on inferential statistics and instead focus on the descriptive pattern and qualitative divergence between actions and beliefs. The observed means also differed from the standard level-0 assumption of choosing the lowest available number (11).\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe observed divergence between beliefs and actions in the 11-20 game variant is significant for level-k modeling because it challenges the assumption that level-0 behavior is both non-strategic and commonly understood by higher-level players. Our findings suggest that level-0 reasoning may be influenced by contextual cues and payoff structures, and that higher-level players may misperceive or mispredict level-0 behavior in certain games. The team-based consensus format, while providing valuable data on explicit reasoning, may also have influenced the results: groupthink or the influence of dominant team members during consensus formation could lead to choices that do not fully reflect individual reasoning. For example, a particularly vocal participant might steer the group toward a salient number, or the desire for agreement might suppress dissenting views, resulting in consensus choices that mask individual variation in level-0 reasoning. These dynamics could either amplify or dampen the expression of non-strategic reasoning, depending on the group composition and interaction style.\n\nTo refine level-k models, researchers might consider incorporating context-dependent definitions of level-0 behavior, such as allowing the focal point or salient features of the game to determine level-0 choices, or modeling heterogeneity in non-strategic reasoning across games. Among alternative explanations, the influence of focal points (e.g., salient numbers like 15 in the 11-20 game) and heuristic-based reasoning (e.g., \"choose a number in the middle\" or \"avoid extremes\") appear particularly promising for capturing observed discrepancies. Future studies could control for group dynamics by comparing team-based and individual formats, or by using structured protocols that minimize the influence of dominant individuals (e.g., requiring private written responses before group discussion).\n\nLimitations\n\nLimitations of our study include the use of a"}}